 ## Overview
-Tetrahedral Projection with Partial Pre-integration (PTINT) is a hybrid volume and isosurface rendering method that projects tetrahedra onto the screen and computes fragment colors using pre-integrated optical depth tables [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of explicit isosurface extraction by computing isosurfaces per fragment within the rendering pipeline, avoiding the case-handling complexity of marching tetrahedra [S1, unofficial]. Unlike full pre-integration, PTINT uses a transfer-function-independent $\psi$ table, allowing interactive transfer function editing without expensive table recomputation [S2, unofficial].
+Tetrahedral Projection with Partial Pre-integration (PTINT) is a hybrid volume and isosurface rendering method that projects tetrahedra onto the screen and computes fragment colors using pre-integrated optical depth tables [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of explicit isosurface extraction by computing isosurfaces per fragment within the rendering pipeline, avoiding the case-handling complexity of marching tetrahedra [S1, unofficial]. Unlike full pre-integration, PTINT uses a transfer-function-independent $\psi$ table, allowing interactive transfer function editing without expensive table recomputation [S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Hardware-Assisted Projected Tetrahedra](1729-hardware-assisted-projected-tetrahedra.md) by leveraging GPU rasterization for cell projection [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Regular 3D scalar volume data (stored as hexahedral volunits), viewing vector $\vec{v}$, isovalue (optional), transfer function (TF), Phong illumination coefficients (optional).
-**Outputs:** Final color buffer.
